## Comparisons

### LangGraph vs. LangChain (for Sequential Workflows)

| Dimension | LangChain (Chains / LCEL) | LangGraph (StateGraph) |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| State Persistence | Intermediate node outputs are frequently discarded or difficult to trace at pipeline termination. | Accumulative Central State: All intermediate outputs (e.g. outline alongside content) persist and remain accessible at the end. |
| Execution Paradigm | Component-to-component pipeline chaining (e.g. Prompt | LLM | Parser). | Graph Architecture: Explicit state schema with Python functions as nodes and directional edges. |
| Role in Agentic AI | Provides atomic primitives: Chat Models, Loaders, Splitters, Prompt Templates. | Orchestrates agentic control flow, decision paths, and state evolution. |
| Suitability for Single Calls | Best suited; minimal overhead for simple 1-step queries (model.invoke(...)). | Overkill for simple single queries, but essential preparation for complex multi-step and cyclic workflows. |

### Non-LLM vs. LLM Workflows in LangGraph

| Dimension | Non-LLM Workflow (e.g. BMI Calculator) | LLM Workflow (e.g. Prompt Chaining) |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| Dependencies | Requires only Python built-ins and langgraph. | Requires langchain, langchain-openai, and API keys via python-dotenv. |
| Node Workload | Deterministic mathematical calculations and conditional rules. | Non-deterministic generative inference via model.invoke(). |
| Execution Latency | Sub-millisecond instantaneous execution. | Multi-second latency driven by sequential API network roundtrips. |
| Primary Pedagogical Purpose | Master syntax, TypedDict, node signatures, and edge wiring. | Master task decomposition, prompt chaining, and real-world multi-stage LLM coordination. |

